<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · 1885 · Central &amp; East Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Battōtai</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ja">抜刀隊</bdi> / <bdi lang="ja-Latn">Battōtai</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Japanese army song of 1885 about a squad of police swordsmen in an 1877 civil war. Its tune is still the march of Japan's army and police.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Toyama Masakazu</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Charles Leroux</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Meiji Japan; the Imperial Japanese Army</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Tokyo, Japan</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>words 1882; song 1885; Army March-Past 1886</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Toyama Masakazu, published 1882; music by Charles Leroux, 1885)</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The Battōtai (&quot;Drawn-Sword Unit&quot;) was a squad of police swordsmen formed by the Meiji government in 1877, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-rebellion">Satsuma Rebellion</a>, the uprising led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori">Saigō Takamori</a>. At the Battle of Tabaruzaka the government's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscript</a> army, mostly commoners who had never learned to fight with the sword, suffered heavy losses in close combat with Saigō's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a>. Officers of the police brigade, many of them of samurai origin from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> itself, proposed to the army commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamagata-aritomo">Yamagata Aritomo</a> that the best swordsmen of the police be sent in; Yamagata, the champion of conscription, agreed reluctantly and named the unit himself. About a hundred men attacked on 14 March 1877. The charge proved decisive for the taking of Tabaruzaka, but the unit lost heavily, by the English article's figures 25 dead and 54 wounded in two days.</p><p>A report by the journalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inukai-tsuyoshi">Inukai Tsuyoshi</a>, then of the Yūbin Hōchi Shimbun, said that former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aizu">Aizu</a>, branded rebels in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a>, charged crying for revenge for that war. The report rested on hearsay and is not in the official records, and the Japanese article notes that the core of the unit was in fact men of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a>, fighting their own countrymen. The unit's success revived interest in swordsmanship, abandoned after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>: Kawaji Toshiyoshi, the founder of the Japanese police, wrote a treatise calling for its revival, and from 1879 the Tokyo police hired fencing instructors, a step toward modern kendo.</p><p>In 1882 Toyama Masakazu, a professor at the University of Tokyo, published a poem on the unit's fight in the collection Shintaishi-shō. Charles Leroux, a French military bandmaster employed by the Japanese government, set it to music, and the war song &quot;Battōtai&quot; was published in 1885. In 1886 its music was combined with the melody of the song &quot;Fusō-ka&quot; to make the Army March-Past (Rikugun bunretsu kōshinkyoku), adopted as the official march of the Imperial Japanese Army. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-war">Pacific War</a> the march was among the pieces used for radio announcements of victories.</p><p>The march is still the official march of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> Ground Self-Defense Force and of the Tokyo Metropolitan Police and the prefectural police forces, played by their bands at reviews. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a> broke out in 1894, Sonoda Yasukata, by then Superintendent-General of the Tokyo police and once one of the unit's officers, proposed sending a new Battōtai to the front; the army's vice chief of staff, Kawakami Sōroku, rejected it as childish, saying that the army had long outgrown such things.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A poem on the fight of the police sword unit at Tabaruzaka in 1877, set as a stirring military song.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Japanese</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Japanese</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">我は官軍我敵は</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">Ware wa kangun waga teki wa</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We are the army of the government; our enemy</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">天地容れざる朝敵ぞ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">tenchi irezaru chōteki zo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>is the enemy of the court, whom heaven and earth will not bear.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">敵の大將たる者は</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">teki no taishō taru mono wa</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The man who commands the enemy</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">古今無雙の英雄で</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">kokon musō no eiyū de</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>is a hero without equal, now or ever,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">之に從ふ兵は</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">kore ni shitagau tsuwamono wa</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and the warriors who follow him</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">共に慓悍決死の士</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">tomo ni hyōkan kesshi no shi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>are all fierce men ready to die.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">鬼神に恥ぬ勇あるも</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">kijin ni hajinu yū aru mo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Though they have a courage that would not shame the gods,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">天の許さぬ叛逆を</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">ten no yurusanu hangyaku o</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>those who have raised a rebellion that heaven does not allow</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">起せし者は昔より</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">okoseshi mono wa mukashi yori</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>have never, from of old,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">榮えし例あらざるぞ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">sakaeshi tameshi arazaru zo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>been known to prosper.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">敵の亡ぶる夫迄は</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">teki no horoburu sore made wa</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Until the enemy is destroyed,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">進めや進め諸共に</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">susume ya susume morotomo ni</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>forward, forward, all together;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">玉ちる劔拔き連れて</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">tama chiru tsurugi nukitsurete</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>with flashing swords drawn side by side,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">死ぬる覺悟で進むべし</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">shinuru kakugo de susumu beshi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we must go forward ready to die.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Toyama Masakazu, in Shintaishi-shō (1882; second printing 1884). Text from <a href="https://ja.wikisource.org/wiki/%E6%96%B0%E4%BD%93%E8%A9%A9%E6%8A%84%2F%E6%8A%9C%E5%88%80%E9%9A%8A%E3%81%AE%E8%A9%A9%EF%BC%88%E4%B8%B6%E5%B1%B1%E4%BB%99%E5%A3%AB%EF%BC%89" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (modified Hepburn).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (5)</summary><ul><li>The first of the poem’s six stanzas; its last four lines are the refrain of every stanza.</li><li>Romanization: modified Hepburn of the words as they are read and sung; the historical kana spelling is not transliterated letter by letter (從ふ shitagau, 榮えし sakaeshi).</li><li>The poem is about the Satsuma Rebellion of 1877: the enemy’s commander is Saigō Takamori, whom it honours as a hero while condemning the rising.</li><li>Line 7: 鬼神 (kijin), fierce gods or spirits.</li><li>Line 13: 玉ちる劔, literally “swords that scatter jewels”, an old phrase for blades that flash.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Army March-Past (Rikugun bunretsu kōshinkyoku)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1886</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as (combined with the melody of Fusō-ka)</span></li></ul></section>
